-- The W1 fixture (BLAWX-EXPORT-SPEC §11 W1, measured 2026-09-02): the FIRST
-- Rock Paper Scissors encoding, the one that reads s.4 the way the Act reads —
-- "the player who throws a sign that beats the sign of the OTHER player" — and
-- finds the other player by comparing two Player records with `EQUALS`.
--
-- It is refused, and the refusal is the point. L4 compares records BY VALUE;
-- Blawx compares objects BY ATOM. R11's query flattening emits one Blawx object
-- per occurrence of a record value, so the `#EVAL` below hands the query a
-- Player object `p1` while the game's `first player` slot is filled by a second
-- object `p2` with the same `throws` — structurally equal, atomically distinct.
-- `P = Firstplayer` then fails, `other player` derives nothing, and `wins`
-- has no model. L4 says TRUE, s(CASP) says "no models": the §3.2.1 failure
-- class — source that parses, checks, and answers DIFFERENTLY — which is
-- visible here only because R11 keeps the oracle outside the artifact.
--
-- Measured that day: this module lowered clean and the tier-1 harness returned
-- no model for the `bobjane` query. Silence is the worst possible outcome for a
-- transpiler, so the lowering now refuses `EQUALS`/disequality on record-typed
-- operands with a named diagnostic (`record identity (Blawx)`).
--
-- What a user does about it: state the rule once per SLOT, which needs no
-- identity at all — `first player wins` / `second player wins`, which is what
-- the shipped `jl4/examples/blawx/rps.l4` does and which passes tier-1 4/4 — or
-- compare an enum- or scalar-valued FIELD (`p's throws EQUALS q's throws`)
-- instead of the records themselves, since enums and numbers ARE atoms in
-- Blawx and do survive the flattening.
--
-- The later fix, deliberately not taken in v1: hash-cons structurally-equal
-- record arguments in the query flattening so one distinct value becomes one
-- object, and lift the refusal. Recorded in §11 W1 as the next step.

§ `Rock Paper Scissors Act`

DECLARE Sign IS ONE OF Rock, Paper, Scissors

DECLARE Player HAS
    throws IS A Sign

DECLARE Game HAS
    `first player`  IS A Player
    `second player` IS A Player

@export 4. The winner of a game is the player who throws a sign that beats the sign of the other player.
GIVEN p IS A Player
      g IS A Game
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `wins` p g IF
  `beats` (p's throws) EQUALS (`other player` g p)'s throws

@export 3. The signs are related in the following ways: (a) Rock beats Scissors, (b) Scissors beats Paper, and (c) Paper beats Rock.
GIVEN s IS A Sign
GIVETH A Sign
DECIDE `beats` s IS
  CONSIDER s
    WHEN Rock THEN Scissors
    WHEN Scissors THEN Paper
    WHEN Paper THEN Rock

@export 1. A game of rock paper scissors has two players. The other player is whichever of the two is not the one in question.
GIVEN g IS A Game
      p IS A Player
GIVETH A Player
DECIDE `other player` g p IS
  IF p EQUALS g's `first player` THEN g's `second player` ELSE g's `first player`

-- Jason's `bobjane` test: jane throws rock, bob throws scissors.
#EVAL `wins` (Player WITH throws IS Rock)
             (Game WITH `first player` IS Player WITH throws IS Rock
                        `second player` IS Player WITH throws IS Scissors)
-- L4 oracle ==> TRUE; tier-1 ==> NO model (the divergence)
